  "articleBody": "Renovating a foreclosed property can be a great investment, but it’s also easy to make missteps that cost you time and money. One of the biggest mistakes is jumping straight to painting or other cosmetic fixes without thoroughly diagnosing underlying issues. Imagine painting over a water stain only to find out later there's an active leak behind the wall – that's a problem! Always check for leaks, wiring issues, and rot *before* you start with finishes. Another common pitfall is underestimating costs. Foreclosed homes often have stripped fixtures, missing appliances, or even vandalized sections, meaning your repair budget might need to be significantly larger than for a standard renovation. Some experts even suggest budgeting 10-25% of the purchase price just for unexpected repairs. Don't treat paint as a quick fix for essential repairs. It's crucial to address structural, plumbing, and electrical problems first. When in doubt, especially with complex issues, bringing in a professional general contractor can save you from costly rework and ensure the job's done right the first time.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}